'''Eran Elinav''' ({{Langx|he|ערן אלינב}}; born 22 June 1969 in Jerusalem, Israel) is an [[Israelis|Israeli]] [[immunologist]]<ref name="nature">{{cite journal |title=No guts, no research glory |journal=Nature Medicine |date=2019 |doi=10.1038/s41591-019-0342-5 |last1=Elinav |first1=Eran |volume=25 |issue=2 |page=196 |pmid=30728539 |s2cid=256840579 |doi-access=free }}</ref><!--<ref name="BBC">{{cite news |last1=Brown |first1=Jessica |title=What we do and don't know about gut health |url=https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20190121-what-we-do-and-dont-know-about-gut-health |accessdate=22 July 2020 |publisher=[[BBC]] |date=22 January 2019}}</ref><ref name="theguardian">{{cite news |last1=Davis |first1=Nicola KS |title=Vaginal fluid transplants could cure bacterial vaginosis |url=https://www.theguardian.com/society/2019/oct/07/vaginal-fluid-transplants-could-cure-bacterial-vaginosis |accessdate=22 July 2020 |publisher=[[The guardian]] |date=7 October 2019}}</ref><ref name="globes">{{cite news |title=Probiotics can be worse than useless, Israeli scientists find |url=https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-probiotics-may-be-worse-than-useless-israeli-scientists-find-1001253002 |accessdate=22 July 2020 |publisher=[[Globes]] |date=6 September 2018}}</ref> --> and microbiota researcher<ref name="JEM" /><!-- <ref name="haaretz">{{cite news |last1=Reisfeld |first1=Smadar |title=Which Foods Make You Fat? The Answer Is in Your Gut |url=https://www.haaretz.com/science-and-health/MAGAZINE-which-foods-are-bad-for-you-the-answer-is-in-your-gut-1.5403241 |accessdate=22 July 2020 |publisher=[[Haaretz]] |date=11 February 2016}}</ref> --> at the [[Weizmann Institute of Science]]<ref name="ElinavLab" /><!-- <ref name="indiatimes">{{cite news |last1=Akhter |first1=Shahid |title=Blood sugar levels response to food are highly individualized: Dr Eran Elinav |url=https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/industry/blood-sugar-levels-response-to-food-are-highly-individualized-/70099863 |accessdate=22 July 2020 |publisher=[[Indiatimes]] |date=6 July 2019}}</ref><ref name="israel21c">{{cite news |last1=Klein Leichman |first1=Abigail |title=Is the microbiome about to change medicine for good? |url=https://www.israel21c.org/is-the-microbiome-about-to-change-medicine-for-good/ |accessdate=22 July 2020 |publisher=israel21c |date=15 October 2019}}</ref> --> and the [[German Cancer Research Center]].<ref name="DKFZ" />  He is an international scholar at the [[Howard Hughes Medical Institute]] and the [[Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation]],<ref name="HHMI">{{cite web |title=Eran Elinav, MD, PhD – HHMI-Gates International Research Scholar |url=https://www.hhmi.org/programs/biomedical-research/international-programs#eran-elinav |website=[[Howard Hughes Medical Institute]] |accessdate=17 July 2020}}</ref> a senior fellow of the [[Canadian Institute for Advanced Research]],<ref name="CIFAR">{{cite web |title=Eran Elinav |url=https://www.cifar.ca/bio/eran-elinav |website=[[Canadian Institute for Advanced Research]] |accessdate=17 July 2020}}</ref> and a partner at [[Novo Nordisk Foundation]] Microbiome Health Initiative.<ref name="Novo">{{cite web |title=Microbiome Health Initiative |url=https://www.linkedin.com/company/nnf-microbiome-health-initiative/ |website=[[Linkedin]] |accessdate=31 December 2025}}</ref>

== Academic and medical career ==

Elinav earned a M.D. from the [[Hebrew University of Jerusalem|Hebrew University in Jerusalem]] in 1999.<ref name=":7">{{Cite web |title=Eran Elinav |url=https://israel-taiwan.tau.ac.il/Eran_Elinav |access-date=2026-09-29 |website=israel-taiwan.tau.ac.il |language=en-US}}</ref> Following an internship and residency in internal medicine at the [[Hadassah Medical Center|Hadassah-Hebrew University Medical Center]] in 2000–2004.<ref name=":7" /><!-- <ref>{{cite web |title=Scientific founders |url=https://www.biomx.com/about/ |website=biomx |accessdate=22 July 2020}}</ref> -->

He served as a senior physician-scientist at the [[Tel Aviv Sourasky Medical Center]] Institute of [[Gastroenterology]] and Liver Disease in 2005–2009.<ref name="JEM">{{cite news |last1=Houston |first1=Stephanie |title=Eran Elinav: Beyond the microbiome |url=https://rupress.org/jem/article/216/1/1/42436/Eran-Elinav-Beyond-the-microbiomeEran-Elinav |accessdate=22 July 2020 |publisher=Journal of Experimental Medicine (JEM) |date=30 November 2018}}</ref><!-- <ref>{{cite web |title=les probiotiques sont-ils vraiment efficaces ? |url=https://www.israelscienceinfo.com/medecine/weizmann-israel-les-probiotiques-sont-ils-vraiment-efficaces/ |website=Israel Science Info |accessdate=22 July 2020}}</ref> -->

In 2009 Elinav earned a Ph.D. in immunology from the [https://www.weizmann.ac.il/ Weizmann Institute of Science], advised by [[Zelig Eshhar]], after developing the Chimeric Antigen Receptor [[Regulatory T cell]] (CAR-Treg) approach,<ref>{{cite journal | last1 = Elinav | first1 = E. | last2 = Waks | first2 = T. | last3 = Eshhar | first3 = Z. | year = 2008 | title = Redirection of regulatory T cells with predetermined specificity for the treatment of experimental colitis in mice | url = | journal = Gastroenterology | volume = 134 | issue = 7| pages = 2014–2024 | pmid = 18424268 | doi = 10.1053/j.gastro.2008.02.060 }}</ref> as treatment of [[inflammatory bowel diseases|inflammatory bowel disease]] and autoimmunity. In 2009-2012 he 
followed by a post-doc at [[Yale University]], advised by [[Richard A. Flavell|Richard Anthony Flavell]], in which he discovered the NLRP6 [[inflammasome]].<ref>{{cite journal | last1 = Elinav | first1 = E. | last2 = Strowig | first2 = T. | last3 = Kau | first3 = A.L. | last4 = Henao-Mejia | first4 = J. | last5 = Thaiss | first5 = C.A. | last6 = Booth | first6 = C.J. | last7 = Peaper | first7 = D.R. | last8 = Bertin | first8 = J. | last9 = Eisenbarth | first9 = S.C. | last10 = Gordon | first10 = J.I. | last11 = Flavell | first11 = R.A. | year = 2011 | title = NLRP6 inflammasome regulates colonic microbial ecology and risk for colitis | journal = Cell | volume = 145 | issue = 5| pages = 745–757 | pmid = 21565393 | doi = 10.1016/j.cell.2011.04.022 | pmc = 3140910 | bibcode = 2011Cell..145..745E }}</ref>

In 2012 Elinav moved to the Weizmann Institute of Science and in 2016 was made a professor.<ref name="ElinavLab">{{cite web |title=ElinavLab |url=https://www.weizmann.ac.il/immunology/elinav/ |website=[[Weizmann Institute of Science]] |accessdate=17 July 2020}}</ref>
He heads the Institute of Microbiome research<ref>{{cite web |title=The Institute for Microbiome Research |url=https://centers.weizmann.ac.il/microbiome/ |website=[[Weizmann Institute of Science]] |accessdate=17 July 2020}}</ref> and the Center of Host-Pathogen Interaction Research at the Weizmann Institute of Science and the Microbiome & Cancer Division at the [[German Cancer Research Center]].<ref name="DKFZ">{{cite web |title=Division of Microbiome and Cancer – Prof. Dr. Eran Elinav |url=https://www.dkfz.de/en/mikrobiom-und-krebs/index.php |website=[[German Cancer Research Center]] |accessdate=17 July 2020}}</ref> He is the head of the [https://www.weizmann.ac.il/immunology/ Department of Systems Immunology] at the Weizmann Institute of Science.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schwartz |first=Amit |date=2025-09-04 |title=Eran Elinav, MD, PhD {{!}} Israel Cancer Research Fund |url=https://www.icrfonline.org/grants/eran-elinav-md-phd/ |access-date=2026-09-30 |language=en-US}}</ref>

